// What `str`'s built-in methods leave out.
//
// API
// ---
//   text.replace(s, from, to, count:)  -> str    every occurrence, or the first `count`
//   text.lines(s)                      -> List<str>  split on \n, \r\n; no empty last line
//   text.split_whitespace(s)           -> List<str>  runs of space; no empty fields
//   text.trim_start(s), trim_end(s)    -> str    one end of `trim`
//   text.strip_prefix(s, p)            -> str    `p` removed if it is there
//   text.strip_suffix(s, p)            -> str
//   text.pad_left(s, width, fill:)     -> str    fill on the LEFT, counted in code points
//   text.pad_right(s, width, fill:)    -> str    fill on the right
//   text.parse_int(s)                  -> Result<int, ParseError>   a parse that says why
//
// **Every function here is one `str` cannot answer.** `str` has fifteen
// methods and a program reaches for them first; this module is the short list
// of questions that come up as often and that a caller would otherwise write a
// byte loop for. It holds no second spelling of something `str` already does:
// there is no `text.upper`, no `text.contains`, no `text.join`.
//
// --- what is deliberately NOT here ------------------------------------------
//
// Each of these was wanted by name and left out, because "a program might" is
// not a reason and the surface is frozen the day it ships.
//
//   `replace_first(s, from, to)`   is `replace(s, from, to, count: 1)`. One
//       function with a bound reads the same and covers `count: 2`, which a
//       second name never would. CPython's `str.replace(old, new, count=-1)`.
//
//   `index_of_from(s, needle, at)` -- the one that hurts to leave out, and the
//       one that would hurt more to add. Written here it can only be
//       `s.substr(at, s.size()).index_of(needle)`, which copies the rest of the
//       string on every call: a loop over every occurrence, the only reason to
//       want it, would be QUADRATIC. A library function whose obvious use is
//       quadratic is worse than no function. The search belongs to the runtime,
//       so the honest form is a built-in `s.index_of(sub, from: i)` -- and
//       until there is one, `s.split(needle)` finds every occurrence in one
//       linear pass, which is what `replace` below does.
//
//   `count(s, needle)`             is `s.split(needle).size() - 1`, one line,
//       and the one line says what it costs. `split` already traps on an empty
//       needle, so the spelling inherits the right answer to the hard case.
//
//   `trim_chars(s, chars)`         is CPython's `strip(chars)`, where `chars`
//       is a SET and not an affix: `"ping.png".strip(".png")` is `"pi"`. Oro
//       keeps it and calls it a trap in the same sentence. What a caller
//       almost always means is `strip_prefix` / `strip_suffix`, which are here.
//
//   `title(s)`, `capitalize(s)`    are case, and case is Unicode's: `"ﬁ"`
//       title-cases to `"Fi"` and `"ǳ"` to `"ǲ"`, neither of which an ASCII
//       table can reach. `str.to_upper` is ASCII and says so; a second ASCII
//       case function would be a second thing to migrate the day
//       `unicode.upper` lands (docs/text-decision.md §8).
//
//   `center(s, width)`, `wrap(s, width)`  need a width in TERMINAL CELLS, not
//       code points: a CJK ideograph is two cells wide, a combining mark is
//       zero, and an emoji sequence is one grapheme of two. That is East Asian
//       Width and UAX #29, which the `unicode` module owns. `pad_left` and
//       `pad_right` below count code points and say so; nothing here pretends
//       to know how wide a character looks.
//
//   `parse_int(s, base:)`, `parse_float` with a reason. A radix has no caller:
//       `json`'s `\u` escape reads exactly four hex digits and wants the
//       position of the bad one, which no whole-string parse can give it. And
//       `parse_float`'s only failures are "not a number" and "too large for a
//       double", the second of which every other language calls `inf` rather
//       than an error -- a "why" for floats would be re-arguing
//       `lib/__floatfmt.src`, not answering a question a caller has.

/// Why `parse_int` refused its input.
///
/// The three ways a decimal integer can fail, which `str.parse_int`'s
/// `Option<int>` flattens into one `None`. That flattening is right for a
/// built-in -- it answers "did it parse" -- and wrong for a program that has
/// to tell a user what to type instead.
///
/// Payload-free (docs/errors-decision.md §4): which byte, where, or which end
/// of the range was passed is context the caller already has -- it has `s`
/// -- when it reports.
pub enum ParseError {
    /// Nothing to parse: the text is empty, or it is a lone `+` or `-`.
    NoDigits;
    /// A byte that is not a decimal digit.
    Digit;
    /// Every character is a digit and the value is outside an `int`'s range.
    Range;
}

/// A sentence saying what was wrong. A method named `to_str`, so
/// `print(e)` prints it.
pub str ParseError.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case NoDigits: {
            return "no digits to parse";
        }
        case Digit: {
            return "invalid digit";
        }
        case Range: {
            return "the number is outside an int's range";
        }
    }
}

// --- replace -----------------------------------------------------------------
/// `s` with every occurrence of `from` swapped for `to`, or with only the
/// first `count` of them if `count` is not negative.
///
///     text.replace("a-b-c", "-", "+")             == "a+b+c"
///     text.replace("a-b-c", "-", "+", count: 1)   == "a+b-c"
///
/// **An empty `from` traps.** CPython replaces between every character --
/// `"ab".replace("", "-")` is `"-a-b-"` -- which is a spelling nobody writes on
/// purpose and every caller who reaches it reached it by accident, with a
/// needle computed from something that turned out to be empty. `str.split`
/// already traps on an empty separator for the same reason (reference §6.5),
/// and this is `split` with the pieces rejoined differently, so it is one rule
/// and not two.
///
/// **Matches are found left to right and do not overlap**, and the text that
/// was replaced is never rescanned: `text.replace("aaa", "aa", "b")` is `"ba"`,
/// and `text.replace("a", "a", "aa")` is `"aa"` and not a loop. Those are
/// CPython's answers too. `from` and `to` may be any text, multi-byte
/// included: a needle is valid UTF-8, so it begins with a non-continuation
/// byte and ends a character, and every match starts and ends on a boundary.
///
/// **Linear, once.** The whole string is cut by `split`, which is the
/// runtime's search, and put back by `join`, which sizes the result once and
/// copies each piece into it. Nothing here appends to a growing `str`, which
/// would copy the result so far at every match and make a long string with
/// many matches quadratic.
pub str replace(str s, str from, str to, int count = -1) {
    if (from == "") {
        trap("text.replace(): the text to replace may not be empty");
    }
    if (count == 0 || !s.contains(from)) {
        return s;
    }
    List<str> parts = s.split(from);
    if (count < 0 || count >= parts.size() - 1) {
        return parts.join(to);
    }
    // A bounded count: the first `count + 1` pieces rejoin with the new text
    // and the rest rejoin with the old, which is what `split` cut them on --
    // so the tail comes back exactly as it went in.
    List<str> head = [];
    List<str> tail = [];
    int i = 0;
    while (i <= count) {
        head.push(parts[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    while (i < parts.size()) {
        tail.push(parts[i]);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return head.join(to) + from + tail.join(from);
}

// --- lines -------------------------------------------------------------------
/// The lines of `s`, without their terminators.
///
///     text.lines("a\nb\n")     == ["a", "b"]
///     text.lines("a\r\nb")     == ["a", "b"]
///     text.lines("")           == []
///
/// This is what `io.read(path)` wants next, and it is the one thing
/// `s.split("\n")` gets wrong three ways.
///
/// **A trailing newline is a terminator, not a separator**: a file ending
/// `"a\n"` has one line, not two. This is Go's `bufio.Scanner` and CPython's
/// `splitlines`, and it is the answer that makes `lines` a right inverse of
/// writing each line with a `"\n"` after it -- which is how every line-oriented
/// file is written. `"a\n\n"` is still two lines, `"a"` and `""`: the empty one
/// is terminated too.
///
/// **`\r\n` is one terminator and is dropped whole.** A `\r` is dropped only
/// when a `\n` follows it, so `"a\r"` at the end of the text is one line
/// holding a carriage return. Rust's `str::lines` draws the line in the same
/// place; Go's `bufio.Scanner` drops that last `\r` too, and CPython's
/// `splitlines` goes further and calls a lone `\r` a terminator of its own.
/// Neither is taken, and consistency is the reason: `"a\rb"` is one line by
/// every reading, so the `\r` in it is data -- and a byte that is data in the
/// middle cannot be a terminator at the end. A program printing a progress bar
/// writes `"50%\r75%\r"` and means one line.
///
/// **Nothing else terminates a line.** CPython's `splitlines` also breaks on
/// `\v`, `\f`, `\x1c`, `\x1d`, `\x1e`, `\x85`, `U+2028` and `U+2029`, so
/// `"a\vb".splitlines()` is two lines. No file format writes those as line
/// breaks, the last three need a Unicode answer this language defers
/// (docs/text-decision.md §8), and a program that meets one in a log file
/// wants it kept, not silently turned into a row.
pub List<str> lines(str s) {
    List<str> parts = s.split("\n");
    List<str> out = [];
    int last = parts.size() - 1;
    for (int i in 0 .. last) {
        str part = parts[i];
        if (part.ends_with("\r")) {
            part = part.substr(0, part.size() - 1);
        }
        out.push(part);
    }
    // What follows the final newline is a line only if there is something in
    // it. `split` always answers at least one piece, so `parts[last]` is safe.
    if (parts[last] != "") {
        out.push(parts[last]);
    }
    return out;
}

// --- whitespace ----------------------------------------------------------------
/// The runs of non-space in `s`, in order.
///
///     text.split_whitespace("  a\tb  c ")   == ["a", "b", "c"]
///     text.split_whitespace("   ")          == []
///
/// For a line of columns written by something else -- `df`, `/proc/meminfo`, a
/// log format that pads. `s.split(" ")` cannot read one: two spaces make an
/// empty field, a tab is not a space at all, and a leading or trailing space
/// makes an empty field at each end. This is CPython's `s.split()` with no
/// separator, Go's `strings.Fields` and Rust's `split_whitespace`; there is no
/// `maxsplit`, because nothing asked for one.
///
/// The space is `str.trim`'s: the six ASCII bytes space, tab, newline,
/// carriage return, form feed and vertical tab, and nothing else. CPython also
/// breaks on `\x1c` to `\x1f` and on every Unicode space; `trim` does not, and
/// two definitions of space in one language would be the worse answer.
pub List<str> split_whitespace(str s) {
    List<str> out = [];
    int n = s.size();
    int i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        while (i < n && is_space(s.byte_at(i))) {
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (i >= n) {
            return out;
        }
        int from = i;
        while (i < n && !is_space(s.byte_at(i))) {
            i = i + 1;
        }
        out.push(s.substr(from, i));
    }
    return out;
}

/// `s` with `str.trim`'s whitespace removed from the front only.
///
///     text.trim_start("  a  ")   == "a  "
///
/// Half of `trim`, which cannot be asked for half. The need is the one every
/// tool that rewrites a text file has: strip what trails a line WITHOUT
/// touching the indentation in front of it. A caller writing it themselves has
/// to rediscover which six bytes `trim` counts -- `\v` and `\f` are the two
/// that get forgotten -- and a program that disagrees with `trim` by one byte
/// disagrees only on input nobody tests.
pub str trim_start(str s) {
    int n = s.size();
    int i = 0;
    while (i < n && is_space(s.byte_at(i))) {
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return s.substr(i, n);
}

/// `s` with `str.trim`'s whitespace removed from the end only.
///
///     text.trim_end("  a  ")   == "  a"
pub str trim_end(str s) {
    int i = s.size();
    while (i > 0 && is_space(s.byte_at(i - 1))) {
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return s.substr(0, i);
}

// --- affixes -------------------------------------------------------------------
/// `s` without `prefix`, if it starts with it; `s` unchanged if it does not.
///
///     text.strip_prefix("--verbose", "--")   == "verbose"
///     text.strip_prefix("verbose", "--")     == "verbose"
///
/// The half of `s.starts_with(p)` that has always been missing: the callers
/// of `starts_with` that go on to remove the prefix write
/// `s.substr(p.size(), s.size())` and have to get the arithmetic right --
/// `lib/fs.src`'s `rmtree` does it twice, with a `+ 1` in one of them for the
/// separator.
///
/// **It answers a `str`, not an `Option<str>`.** Rust's `strip_prefix` gives an
/// `Option` and is right to, because it is the only spelling it has; here
/// `starts_with` is already the question "is it there", so an `Option` would
/// make the caller who has just asked ask again. "Remove it if it is there" is
/// the whole meaning of the name. CPython's `removeprefix` and Oro's
/// `rm_prefix` return the string.
pub str strip_prefix(str s, str prefix) {
    if (!s.starts_with(prefix)) {
        return s;
    }
    return s.substr(prefix.size(), s.size());
}

/// `s` without `suffix`, if it ends with it; `s` unchanged if it does not.
///
///     text.strip_suffix("notes.txt", ".txt")   == "notes"
pub str strip_suffix(str s, str suffix) {
    if (!s.ends_with(suffix)) {
        return s;
    }
    return s.substr(0, s.size() - suffix.size());
}

// --- padding -------------------------------------------------------------------
/// `s` with `fill` in front of it until it is `width` code points wide.
///
///     text.pad_left("7", 3)              == "  7"
///     text.pad_left("7", 3, fill: "0")   == "007"
///
/// **The name says where the FILL goes**, so `pad_left` is what right-aligns.
/// CPython spells this `rjust`, naming where the text goes, and the pair is
/// the most misremembered in its string API; Go's `text/tabwriter` and every
/// `printf` say `%-5s` for the other one, which is no clearer. One adjective,
/// used for one thing.
///
/// **Width is in code points**, `s.chars().size()`, not bytes: padding exists
/// to line a column up, and bytes misalign every name that is not ASCII --
/// `"café"` is five bytes and four characters. `lib/args.src`'s help table
/// measures the same way.
///
/// That is as far as this goes, and it is not the same as terminal width: a
/// CJK ideograph occupies two cells, a combining mark none, and `"👍🏽"` is two
/// code points a reader sees as one. A table that must line up in a terminal
/// needs East Asian Width and grapheme clusters, which are the `unicode`
/// module's (docs/text-decision.md §8) -- and when it exists, this function
/// does not change: it will still be the code point answer, next to a
/// `unicode` one that counts cells.
///
/// **Never truncates.** An `s` already `width` wide or wider comes back
/// untouched; a column that has to hold `"--jobs"` is not improved by being
/// told `"--job"`. `fill` must be exactly one code point, or it traps: a
/// two-character fill cannot reach every width, so there is no answer to give.
pub str pad_left(str s, int width, str fill = " ") {
    return filling(s, width, fill, "pad_left") + s;
}

/// `s` with `fill` after it until it is `width` code points wide. The
/// rules are `pad_left`'s.
///
///     text.pad_right("--help", 10) + "|"   == "--help    |"
pub str pad_right(str s, int width, str fill = " ") {
    return s + filling(s, width, fill, "pad_right");
}

// What has to be added to `s` to make it `width` code points wide, and nothing
// if it is already. `who` is the caller's name, so the trap says which of the
// two was written rather than naming both.
str filling(str s, int width, str fill, str who) {
    if (fill.chars().size() != 1) {
        trap("text." + who + "(): fill must be one character, not \"" + fill + "\"");
    }
    int have = s.chars().size();
    if (have >= width) {
        return "";
    }
    return fill.repeat(width - have);
}

// --- parsing -------------------------------------------------------------------
/// The decimal integer `s` spells, or why it is not one.
///
///     text.parse_int("42")      -> Ok(42)
///     text.parse_int("4 2")     -> Err(Digit(1, 32))
///     text.parse_int("")        -> Err(NoDigits)
///
/// The same grammar `s.parse_int()` accepts and no other: an optional `+` or
/// `-`, then one or more decimal digits, then the end -- no space, no
/// underscores, no `0x`. What it adds is the reason, which
/// `docs/stdlib-decision.md` named as this module's motivation: the built-in
/// answers "did it parse", and a program reading a config file or a form field
/// has to tell someone what to write instead.
///
/// The grammar is checked here and the arithmetic is not: the value comes from
/// `s.parse_int()`, so the boundary between `Ok` and `Range` is exactly the
/// built-in's and cannot drift from it. A `Range` is therefore reported for
/// precisely the strings the built-in calls `None` after this function has
/// agreed every character is a digit.
pub Result<int, ParseError> parse_int(str s) {
    int n = s.size();
    int i = 0;
    if (n > 0 && (s.byte_at(0) == '+' || s.byte_at(0) == '-')) {
        i = 1;
    }
    if (i >= n) {
        return Result<int, ParseError>.Err(ParseError.NoDigits);
    }
    while (i < n) {
        int c = s.byte_at(i);
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            return Result<int, ParseError>.Err(ParseError.Digit);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    match (s.parse_int()) {
        case Some(int v): {
            return Result<int, ParseError>.Ok(v);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<int, ParseError>.Err(ParseError.Range);
        }
    }
}

// --- shared --------------------------------------------------------------------
// The six bytes `str.trim` treats as space: the runtime's `is_space`
// (runtime/rt.c). Written out because a scan compares a byte, and pinned to
// `trim` rather than trusted: corpus/modules/stdlib-text checks all 128 ASCII
// bytes and fails if one of them vanishes under `trim` and not here, or the
// other way about.
bool is_space(int b) {
    return b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 10 || b == 13 || b == 12 || b == 11;
}
